CAT = "cpctaVitals"; SEG = "VIT"
# Domain 1 tasks 1E, 1F, 1H, 1M to 1T; knowledge k7, k8, k9, k29 to k33, k51 to k53.
Q = [
("An adult's resting pulse is 118 beats per minute. How does this compare with the normal adult range?",
 "Above the normal range", ["Within the normal range", "Below the normal range", "Normal for exercise only"],
 "A normal resting adult pulse is 60 to 100 beats a minute, so 118 is high (tachycardia) and is reported to the nurse."),
("A resting adult takes 9 breaths in one full minute. How is this rate described?",
 "Slower than normal", ["Within normal limits", "Faster than normal", "Normal for an adult at rest"],
 "A resting adult usually breathes about 12 to 18 or 20 times a minute, so 9 is slow (bradypnea) and is reported to the nurse."),
("Where is the patient's arm held while the technician takes a blood pressure?",
 "Supported at heart level", ["Hanging down at the side", "Raised above the head", "Resting on the lap, bent"],
 "The arm is supported at about heart level. An arm below the heart reads falsely high and an arm above it reads falsely low."),
("A patient on dialysis has an arteriovenous fistula in the right forearm. Which arm is used for blood pressure?",
 "The left arm", ["The right arm", "The arm with the fistula", "Whichever arm is closer"],
 "A cuff over a dialysis fistula or graft can damage it, so blood pressure is taken on the other arm."),
("A thin patient's blood pressure is taken with a large thigh-sized cuff on the upper arm. What is the likely effect on the reading?",
 "Falsely low", ["Falsely high", "No effect at all", "A faster pulse"],
 "A cuff that is too large for the arm spreads the pressure over a wider area and tends to give a falsely low reading. The bladder should circle most of the arm."),
("To check for orthostatic changes, the nurse asks for blood pressure lying, sitting and standing. What finding is reported?",
 "A drop when he stands up", ["The same reading each time", "A slight rise when he sits", "A steady pulse throughout"],
 "Orthostatic hypotension is a fall in blood pressure on standing, often with dizziness and a faster pulse. It raises fall risk and is reported to the nurse."),
("A pulse oximeter on a patient's finger reads poorly. Which factor can cause a false reading?",
 "Dark nail polish", ["A warm hand", "A clean fingertip", "A resting patient"],
 "Dark nail polish, cold fingers, poor circulation and movement can all interfere with the light sensor. A warm, still, clean finger gives the best reading."),
("Which temperature route is the least invasive and quick for an alert adult who just drank hot coffee?",
 "Temporal artery", ["Oral, right away", "Rectal", "Axillary under a blanket"],
 "Drinking hot or cold fluids changes an oral reading for a short time. A temporal artery scan is quick and not affected by the drink."),
("Which pulse site is used when checking an adult for a pulse during CPR?",
 "Carotid", ["Radial", "Pedal", "Brachial"],
 "The carotid pulse in the neck is used to check an adult in an emergency. The brachial pulse is used for infants."),
("A patient who cannot stand must be weighed daily for heart failure. Which scale is used?",
 "A bed scale", ["A standing scale", "A baby scale", "A kitchen scale"],
 "A bed scale or sling scale weighs a patient who cannot stand. Daily weights are taken at the same time, on the same scale and in similar clothing."),
("A patient with heart failure gains 1.5 kg since yesterday's weight. Why does this matter?",
 "It may mean fluid retention", ["It shows better nutrition", "It is a scale error only", "It means the diet is working"],
 "A quick gain over a day or two in heart failure usually means fluid is building up, not body tissue, so it is reported to the nurse."),
("A young child cannot rate pain with numbers. Which tool fits her best?",
 "A faces pain scale", ["A 0 to 10 number scale", "A blood pressure reading", "A Glasgow scale"],
 "A faces scale lets a child or a patient with limited language point to a face that matches the pain. A number scale needs the patient to understand numbers."),
("A patient rates his pain 8 out of 10 but is smiling and joking. What does the technician do?",
 "Record and report the 8", ["Record 3 since he looks fine", "Ask him to rate it again later", "Leave the score blank today"],
 "Pain is what the patient says it is. The technician records the patient's own rating and reports it; it is not changed to match how he looks."),
("When pressing on a patient's swollen ankle, the finger leaves a dent that stays. What is this called?",
 "Pitting edema", ["Cellulitis", "Cyanosis", "A skin hematoma"],
 "Pitting edema is swelling from fluid in the tissue where a pressed finger leaves a pit. It is reported to the nurse."),
("A patient was talking normally at breakfast. Now he cannot lift his left arm and his words are jumbled. Why does the time matter?",
 "Stroke treatment is time-sensitive", ["It sets his next meal time", "It decides his bath time", "It only matters for charting"],
 "Sudden weakness on one side and speech changes are stroke warning signs. Treatment depends on when symptoms began, so the technician notes the time last seen normal and calls for help at once."),
("A diabetic patient's point-of-care glucose is 52 mg/dL. What does the technician do?",
 "Tell the nurse now", ["Record it and keep rounding", "Wait to see if it rises", "Tell the nurse at shift change"],
 "A glucose of 52 mg/dL is low (hypoglycemia) and can become dangerous fast, so it is reported to the nurse right away."),
("During purposeful hourly rounding, which items are checked?",
 "Pain, position, toileting, reach", ["Pulse, pressure, pupils, pallor", "Phone, papers, pillows, plants", "Payment, privacy, pharmacy, doctor"],
 "Purposeful rounding checks pain, positioning, personal needs such as toileting, and that possessions and the call light are within reach. It lowers falls and call lights."),
("A technician has four tasks at once. Which should come first?",
 "A patient short of breath", ["A routine 1600 vital signs check", "Refilling a water pitcher", "Changing a clean bed"],
 "Breathing problems threaten life and come first. Routine tasks wait until the urgent need is met and the nurse is told."),
("A wound dressing over a surgical incision shows new redness, warmth and thick yellow drainage. What do these signs suggest?",
 "A wound infection", ["Normal healing", "A dry wound", "An allergy to tape"],
 "Redness, warmth, swelling, pus and fever are signs of infection, which is reported to the nurse."),
("A patient's urine in the collection bag is bright red. What is the next step?",
 "Save it for the nurse to see", ["Empty it and record the amount", "Ask if he ate beets later", "Wait for the next void"],
 "Blood in the urine is an abnormal finding. The specimen is kept so the nurse can see it, and it is reported right away."),
("Which stool appearance should be reported as a possible sign of bleeding in the upper digestive tract?",
 "Black and tarry", ["Soft and brown", "Formed and tan", "Light brown and soft"],
 "Black, tarry stool can mean digested blood from the stomach or upper bowel and is reported. Some iron supplements also darken stool, which the nurse can check."),
("A patient is coughing up pink, frothy sputum. What does the technician do?",
 "Report it at once", ["Tell the nurse at end of shift", "Give the patient a cough drop", "Ask him to cough harder"],
 "Pink, frothy sputum can be a sign of fluid in the lungs and is reported right away, especially in a patient with heart failure."),
("A patient's systolic blood pressure is 82 mmHg and she says she feels faint. What does the technician do?",
 "Keep her lying down", ["Have her stand and walk", "Record it and leave the room", "Recheck it tomorrow morning"],
 "A low blood pressure with faintness puts the patient at risk of falling. Keep her lying down and report the reading to the nurse at once."),
("Before using an electronic thermometer probe for an oral temperature, what does the technician put on it?",
 "A probe cover", ["A drop of lubricant", "An alcohol wipe only", "A glove finger"],
 "A new disposable probe cover is used for each patient to prevent spreading germs. Lubricant is used only for rectal temperatures."),
]
